      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "The fifth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{5}(x)=6+12(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+4(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}(x-1)^{4}+\\frac{3}{10}(x-1)^{5}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(5)}(1)?$\n\n${f}^{(5)}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x-1)^{5}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(1)}{5!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(1)}{5!} & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(5)}(1)}{120} & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(1) & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
